{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was born around 1140 and was still a minor when his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was known to be a capable and intelligent leader.\n\nAfter his regency ended Raymond III became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a cousin of King Baldwin IV and played a significant role in the governance of the kingdom. *Reign as Count of Tripoli* As Count of Tripoli Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to maintain the security of his county. He was also known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to form alliances with neighboring rulers.\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III was appointed as regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. *Regency for Baldwin IV* During his regency Raymond III worked to strengthen the kingdom's defenses and to secure its borders. He also worked to maintain the balance of power within the kingdom and to prevent the rise of any one faction or individual.\n\nRaymond III's regency ended in 1176 when Baldwin IV came of age. However he continued to play a significant role in the kingdom's politics. He was a key advisor to the king and was often called upon to mediate disputes and to provide counsel. Raymond III was also a military leader and fought in several battles against the forces of Saladin.\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III was one of the leaders of the Christian forces at the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Christians and Raymond III was one of the few leaders who managed to escape. He later died in 1187 possibly due to injuries sustained during the battle or due to illness. Raymond III was su